From 635e7df0115cbc7f66197dcb78ff39ee7c961894 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:13:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 106/119] fix: the fog, the clouds and the snow follow the
 aperture too

The rest of the shadow fillers. Four subsystems, one symptom each time:
something bright in the picture that did not move when the aperture did.

Global fog. Its colour is authored - base, height delta and radial all come off
the time of day, none of them is derived from the sun - so it is the same class
of quantity as the skybox emittance and has to follow the aperture like one. It
did not, and fog at distance is often the brightest thing in shadow: it pinned
the shadow values and a night scene washed out to a flat grey veil. One multiply
inside GetVolumetricFogColorInternal, which is where every consumer meets it -
the fog pass, forward shading, the volumetric blend - rather than at the three
CPU constants, because the height delta is a DIFFERENCE of two colours and
scaling it separately is only safe while that recombination stays linear.
Density and falloff are transmittances and are untouched.

Volumetric fog. Its light list already carried the exposure and its sun did not,
so turning the aperture changed the fog's colour BALANCE between its sun-lit and
its lamp-lit halves - a units tear inside one subsystem. The sun now takes the
multiply, next to the PI gate; so does a fog volume's authored emission, for the
reason S1 settled for emissive materials. The scattering albedo does not. And
the ambient-light bug that was fixed in the deferred twin last round was here
too, unfixed, for the same reason: an ambient light is a multiplicative tint over
an already-exposed accumulator, so pre-exposing it applies the exposure twice
inside the shader's lerp and moves its darken/brighten decision boundary.

Clouds. The cloud shading sun colour is a radiance and did not follow, so the sky
darkened when stopping down and the clouds did not - a hard bright rim at every
cloud edge. Both consumers take the multiply (the volumetric stage and the legacy
forward path) rather than the single CPU fill the decision note proposed: that
fill is ReadPerFrameShaderConstants, which runs on the main thread, and the
pre-exposure latch is a render-thread value. The atmospheric scattering intensity
is deliberately left alone - it is a coefficient, not a radiance.

Snow. CSnowStage is the one scene writer that runs after the 709 -> AP1
conversion, and the flake's own lighting term was being taken from the per-view
sun and sky colours: Rec.709, and never pre-exposed. It carries the conversion
itself now, as a delta-from-identity matrix in its own constant buffer, and the
pre-exposure with it. Separately, the half-res composite's saturate(x10) is gone
under the switch: on the stock path clamping at 1.0 was a display-referred
nicety, here 1.0 is 10 000 cd/m2 and that line crushed everything behind the snow
layer - upstream of bloom, the glare family and the meter - to a flat plateau.
The x10 stays; only the clamp becomes the writer clamp every other fp16 writer
already answers to, and the alpha keeps its saturate because it is a blend weight.

r_HDRDebug 1's coverage table now reports all four as reached.

decisions/s8-svogi-fog-clouds.md items 2-5, research/s8-fog-and-clouds.md,
research/s8-particles-rain-lensoptics.md 2.3(c). Off the switch every multiply is
an exact 1.0, the matrix delta is an exact 0, and the clamp branch folds away.

